{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794858063668,"sessionID":"ses_e1a303973ffeu6SRRIvgAvpDYA","part":{"partID":"prt_1e5e03dee0029Mn5kXi5zUnSqH","sessionID":"ses_e1a303973ffeu6SRRIvgAvpDYA","messageID":"msg_1e5e032e0001Cc6jrKJkcTT4zz","type":"tool","id":"call_fe157be5312c4883bc42442d","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Review Iteration 002 - mention-12\n\n## Dispatcher\n\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage.\n- Session: `fanout-mention-12-1794856957949-n58dw7`, generation 1, lineage `mention-12`.\n- Iteration: 2 of 8. Dimensions: security and maintainability. Focus: the untrusted-prompt surface of the mention guard, and the two divergent \"is this command named\" boundary rules.\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass twelve's brief asks for a wrapper, quote or alias case the invariance test does not cover that changes a refusal or question into a request or back. This iteration checked the security side of that question (can a crafted wrapper or homoglyph make the guard offer a command it should withhold, or make it slow) and the maintainability side (does the typed-command reader apply the same wrapper rules as the mention reader). The security probes are clean; the maintainability probe found F003.\n\n## Files Reviewed\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-549`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:196-264` (focused reread of `typedCommandIn`, `typedCommandForTop`, `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY`, `MODE_SUFFIX` and `RETIRED_COMMANDS`)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:410-459` (focused reread of the mention normalizer)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:476-555` (typed-command boundary cases)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439` (the `attachTypedCommand` consumer)\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1200-1260` (the scorer consumers)\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-12.txt:1-10`\n\n## Findings - New\n\n### P0 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P1 Findings\n\nNone.\n\n### P2 Findings\n\n3. **The typed-command reader applies a narrower wrapper set than the mention reader** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:208` -- `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY` admits only whitespace, backtick, straight quotes and an open parenthesis before a command name, while `commandOnlyMentioned` also drops asterisks and curly double quotes at line 427. A command the reader typed inside markdown bold or curly quotes is therefore not \"typed\" for `typedCommandIn`, and both consumers withhold the command: `attachCommand`'s deep-loop gate at line 527 requires `typedCommandIn(prompt) === commands[workflowMode]`, and `typedCommandForTop` at line 256 returns `undefined` before `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:427` can attach it.\n\n   - Case: `attachCommand('system-deep-loop', SINGLE('review'), '**/deep:review** the diff')` returns `undefined`, while `'/deep:review the diff'` returns `'/deep:review'`. `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', 'run **/create:readme** for the advisor skill')` returns `undefined`, while `'run /create:readme for the advisor skill'` returns `{command:'/create:readme',policy:'start',ownerSkillId:'sk-doc'}`. Curly double quotes reproduce both. The wrapped mention itself is read correctly by `commandOnlyMentioned`, so the two readers disagree on the same prompt.\n   - Finding class: cross-consumer\n   - Scope proof: exercised the exported functions on the bold, curly-quote, bracket and parenthesis forms; the parenthesis form already works (`(/deep:review)` delivers) while bold and curly do not, which isolates the missing characters to `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY`. `typedCommandForTop` and the deep-loop gate are the two consumers, and both were run.\n   - Affected surface hints: `lib/command-attach.ts` `typedCommandIn`/`typedCommandForTop`; `lib/command-attach.ts:527` deep-loop gate; `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439`; `tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:476-555`.\n\n## Traceability Checks\n\n- `spec_code` (core): pass. The scope files are unchanged by this iteration; the branch's mention fix is intact (iteration 1 evidence). F003 is a pre-existing asymmetry in the typed path that the pass-eleven wrapper test does not cover, because that test asserts `commandOnlyMentioned` parity only (`[SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:183-188]`).\n- `checklist_evidence` (core): notApplicable. No packet checklist belongs to this review pass.\n- `skill_agent` (overlay): pass. No agent definition is touched.\n- `agent_cross_runtime` (overlay): notApplicable. No runtime mirror is touched.\n- `feature_catalog_code` (overlay): partial. The wrapper contract is implemented twice with different character sets; F003 names the second implementation.\n- `playbook_capability` (overlay): partial. No playbook scenario was executed; the functions were exercised directly.\n\n## Integration Evidence\n\n- `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439` `attachTypedCommand` calls `typedCommandForTop` and returns the output unchanged when it is `undefined`, so the top recommendation loses its `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` fields for a wrapped typed command.\n- `lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1230-1232` `withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit` also compares `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` with the row base, so a bold-wrapped typed command cannot use the \"typed and asked for\" exception on a no-edit prompt.\n- `lib/command-attach.ts:527` the deep-loop gate treats \"typed this command\" as the ask; a wrapped typed command falls through to the depth-wording list instead.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- The deep-loop impact is partly masked by depth wording: `**/deep:review** the diff with a thorough pass` still offers `/deep:review` because `thorough` satisfies `DEPTH_REQUEST`, so the loss shows only on a short typed request.\n- Parenthesized typed commands already work (`(/deep:review)`), so the defect is not \"all markup\"; it is the characters missing from the boundary class.\n- The bracket form `[/deep:review]` fails at both readers: `commandOnlyMentioned` treats a bracketed mention as unled (F001) and `typedCommandIn` does not see a boundary. It is counted under F001, not here.\n- Refusal phrasings outside the curated lead vocabulary (`under no circumstances run /x`, `kindly refrain from running /x`) read as requests. This is the finite-vocabulary design recorded in the module comment at lines 369-377, not a new wrapper or alias case, so it is recorded here and not reported.\n\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\n\n- No prompt text reaches a `RegExp` constructor: the lead and name patterns are built from module constants and `escapePhrase` escapes every command name from the tracked tables.\n- DoS: a 100 KB prompt with one occurrence evaluates in 3.3 ms; 2,000 occurrences in 52 KB evaluate in 289 ms, which is linear in occurrences and not an amplification.\n- No secret, path or filesystem surface: the module reads only its arguments.\n- Homoglyph and zero-width forms do not name a tracked command, so they cannot route to the command whose guard they would bypass.\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- A ReDoS or super-linear scan from prompt-controlled repetition (measured above).\n- A bypass that offers a mutating command from a prompt that never names a tracked command: the homoglyph, zero-width, fullwidth-slash and case variants do not match the tracked tables, so no route selects the command.\n- `escapePhrase` drift: the command names come from trusted tables and every metacharacter in them is escaped before the regex is built.\n- A regression in the retired-name or single-quote behavior of the typed path: `typedCommandIn('/create:skill:auto')` still returns `/create:sk-skill`.\n\n## Next Focus\n\n- Dimension: all four, as a final cross-reference and verification pass\n- Focus area: verify F001, F002 and F003 still reproduce from the final tree, recheck the recorded limits, and prove the lineage state (iteration file, delta, state log, registry, strategy) agrees before synthesis.\n- Reason: security and maintainability are closed; no P0 exists and the active P1 is F001, so the next pass is verification rather than another discovery angle.\n- Rotation status: all four dimensions will be covered after this pass.\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive was exercising the exported functions over concrete prompts; carry that into the final recheck.\n- Required evidence: the same prompts used in iterations 1 and 2, plus the convergence script's stop decision recorded in the state log.\n- Recovery note: none.\n\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\n","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/iterations/iteration-002.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/iterations/iteration-002.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/iterations/iteration-002.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\":\"# Review Iteration 002 - mention-12\\n\\n## Dispatcher\\n\\n- Mode: review. Target agent: `@deep-review`, executed in-process by this detached fan-out lineage.\\n- Session: `fanout-mention-12-1794856957949-n58dw7`, generation 1, lineage `mention-12`.\\n- Iteration: 2 of 8. Dimensions: security and maintainability. Focus: the untrusted-prompt surface of the mention guard, and the two divergent \\\"is this command named\\\" boundary rules.\\n- Operator review focus, and how this iteration addressed it: pass twelve's brief asks for a wrapper, quote or alias case the invariance test does not cover that changes a refusal or question into a request or back. This iteration checked the security side of that question (can a crafted wrapper or homoglyph make the guard offer a command it should withhold, or make it slow) and the maintainability side (does the typed-command reader apply the same wrapper rules as the mention reader). The security probes are clean; the maintainability probe found F003.\\n\\n## Files Reviewed\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:1-549`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:196-264` (focused reread of `typedCommandIn`, `typedCommandForTop`, `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY`, `MODE_SUFFIX` and `RETIRED_COMMANDS`)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:410-459` (focused reread of the mention normalizer)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:476-555` (typed-command boundary cases)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439` (the `attachTypedCommand` consumer)\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1200-1260` (the scorer consumers)\\n- `specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/review-brief-12.txt:1-10`\\n\\n## Findings - New\\n\\n### P0 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P1 Findings\\n\\nNone.\\n\\n### P2 Findings\\n\\n3. **The typed-command reader applies a narrower wrapper set than the mention reader** -- `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts:208` -- `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY` admits only whitespace, backtick, straight quotes and an open parenthesis before a command name, while `commandOnlyMentioned` also drops asterisks and curly double quotes at line 427. A command the reader typed inside markdown bold or curly quotes is therefore not \\\"typed\\\" for `typedCommandIn`, and both consumers withhold the command: `attachCommand`'s deep-loop gate at line 527 requires `typedCommandIn(prompt) === commands[workflowMode]`, and `typedCommandForTop` at line 256 returns `undefined` before `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:427` can attach it.\\n\\n   - Case: `attachCommand('system-deep-loop', SINGLE('review'), '**/deep:review** the diff')` returns `undefined`, while `'/deep:review the diff'` returns `'/deep:review'`. `typedCommandForTop('sk-doc', 'run **/create:readme** for the advisor skill')` returns `undefined`, while `'run /create:readme for the advisor skill'` returns `{command:'/create:readme',policy:'start',ownerSkillId:'sk-doc'}`. Curly double quotes reproduce both. The wrapped mention itself is read correctly by `commandOnlyMentioned`, so the two readers disagree on the same prompt.\\n   - Finding class: cross-consumer\\n   - Scope proof: exercised the exported functions on the bold, curly-quote, bracket and parenthesis forms; the parenthesis form already works (`(/deep:review)` delivers) while bold and curly do not, which isolates the missing characters to `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY`. `typedCommandForTop` and the deep-loop gate are the two consumers, and both were run.\\n   - Affected surface hints: `lib/command-attach.ts` `typedCommandIn`/`typedCommandForTop`; `lib/command-attach.ts:527` deep-loop gate; `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439`; `tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:476-555`.\\n\\n## Traceability Checks\\n\\n- `spec_code` (core): pass. The scope files are unchanged by this iteration; the branch's mention fix is intact (iteration 1 evidence). F003 is a pre-existing asymmetry in the typed path that the pass-eleven wrapper test does not cover, because that test asserts `commandOnlyMentioned` parity only (`[SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-attach-guards.vitest.ts:183-188]`).\\n- `checklist_evidence` (core): notApplicable. No packet checklist belongs to this review pass.\\n- `skill_agent` (overlay): pass. No agent definition is touched.\\n- `agent_cross_runtime` (overlay): notApplicable. No runtime mirror is touched.\\n- `feature_catalog_code` (overlay): partial. The wrapper contract is implemented twice with different character sets; F003 names the second implementation.\\n- `playbook_capability` (overlay): partial. No playbook scenario was executed; the functions were exercised directly.\\n\\n## Integration Evidence\\n\\n- `handlers/advisor-recommend.ts:421-439` `attachTypedCommand` calls `typedCommandForTop` and returns the output unchanged when it is `undefined`, so the top recommendation loses its `command`, `policy` and `ownerSkillId` fields for a wrapped typed command.\\n- `lib/scorer/fusion.ts:1230-1232` `withholdWritingCommandsOnNoEdit` also compares `typedCommandIn(promptLower)` with the row base, so a bold-wrapped typed command cannot use the \\\"typed and asked for\\\" exception on a no-edit prompt.\\n- `lib/command-attach.ts:527` the deep-loop gate treats \\\"typed this command\\\" as the ask; a wrapped typed command falls through to the depth-wording list instead.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- The deep-loop impact is partly masked by depth wording: `**/deep:review** the diff with a thorough pass` still offers `/deep:review` because `thorough` satisfies `DEPTH_REQUEST`, so the loss shows only on a short typed request.\\n- Parenthesized typed commands already work (`(/deep:review)`), so the defect is not \\\"all markup\\\"; it is the characters missing from the boundary class.\\n- The bracket form `[/deep:review]` fails at both readers: `commandOnlyMentioned` treats a bracketed mention as unled (F001) and `typedCommandIn` does not see a boundary. It is counted under F001, not here.\\n- Refusal phrasings outside the curated lead vocabulary (`under no circumstances run /x`, `kindly refrain from running /x`) read as requests. This is the finite-vocabulary design recorded in the module comment at lines 369-377, not a new wrapper or alias case, so it is recorded here and not reported.\\n\\n## Confirmed-Clean Surfaces\\n\\n- No prompt text reaches a `RegExp` constructor: the lead and name patterns are built from module constants and `escapePhrase` escapes every command name from the tracked tables.\\n- DoS: a 100 KB prompt with one occurrence evaluates in 3.3 ms; 2,000 occurrences in 52 KB evaluate in 289 ms, which is linear in occurrences and not an amplification.\\n- No secret, path or filesystem surface: the module reads only its arguments.\\n- Homoglyph and zero-width forms do not name a tracked command, so they cannot route to the command whose guard they would bypass.\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- A ReDoS or super-linear scan from prompt-controlled repetition (measured above).\\n- A bypass that offers a mutating command from a prompt that never names a tracked command: the homoglyph, zero-width, fullwidth-slash and case variants do not match the tracked tables, so no route selects the command.\\n- `escapePhrase` drift: the command names come from trusted tables and every metacharacter in them is escaped before the regex is built.\\n- A regression in the retired-name or single-quote behavior of the typed path: `typedCommandIn('/create:skill:auto')` still returns `/create:sk-skill`.\\n\\n## Next Focus\\n\\n- Dimension: all four, as a final cross-reference and verification pass\\n- Focus area: verify F001, F002 and F003 still reproduce from the final tree, recheck the recorded limits, and prove the lineage state (iteration file, delta, state log, registry, strategy) agrees before synthesis.\\n- Reason: security and maintainability are closed; no P0 exists and the active P1 is F001, so the next pass is verification rather than another discovery angle.\\n- Rotation status: all four dimensions will be covered after this pass.\\n- Blocked/productive carry-forward: productive was exercising the exported functions over concrete prompts; carry that into the final recheck.\\n- Required evidence: the same prompts used in iterations 1 and 2, plus the convergence script's stop decision recorded in the state log.\\n- Recovery note: none.\\n\\nReview verdict: CONDITIONAL\\n\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/iterations/iteration-002.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794858040814,"end":1794858063668}}}}
