{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794858099387,"sessionID":"ses_e1a303973ffeu6SRRIvgAvpDYA","part":{"partID":"prt_1e5e102f0001Uq5DQgR2yx6lQB","sessionID":"ses_e1a303973ffeu6SRRIvgAvpDYA","messageID":"msg_1e5e0fd00001S5TWZU8juYTJNI","type":"tool","id":"call_bc09bab7b44e4094a7abd4da","tool":"edit","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"newString":"- Iteration 1: reading the occurrence scan and the lead tests separately. It showed the bracketed name is found and the lead is lost, which separated F001 (strip list) from F002 (occurrence boundary) instead of merging them.\n- Iteration 2: comparing the two \"is this command named\" readers on the same wrapped prompt. The parenthesized form already worked, which proved the missing characters are confined to `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY` rather than a general markup failure.\n- Iteration 2: measuring the scan on large prompts (100 KB with one occurrence, 2,000 occurrences in 52 KB) instead of asserting a DoS; both are linear and cheap.\n\n---\n\n## 9. WHAT FAILED\n\n- Init: none.\n- Iteration 1: a first read treated the invariance test's \"every fixture\" comment as full coverage; it is full coverage of the six listed marks only. The wrapper set is the boundary, not the fixture list.\n- Iteration 1: the `PLAIN_ROWS` omission of the or-list question, bare-lead, pronoun and later-clause rows looked like a separate coverage gap, but all those rows show 0 mismatches under the covered wrappers, so it was ruled out rather than reported.\n- Iteration 2: the deep-loop half of F003 is masked whenever the prompt also carries depth wording, so the first probe (`**/deep:review** the diff with a thorough pass`) offered the command and had to be narrowed to a short typed request before the loss showed.","oldString":"- Iteration 1: reading the occurrence scan and the lead tests separately. It showed the bracketed name is found and the lead is lost, which separated F001 (strip list) from F002 (occurrence boundary) instead of merging them.\n\n---\n\n## 9. WHAT FAILED\n\n- Init: none.\n- Iteration 1: a first read treated the invariance test's \"every fixture\" comment as full coverage; it is full coverage of the six listed marks only. The wrapper set is the boundary, not the fixture list.\n- Iteration 1: the `PLAIN_ROWS` omission of the or-list question, bare-lead, pronoun and later-clause rows looked like a separate coverage gap, but all those rows show 0 mismatches under the covered wrappers, so it was ruled out rather than reported.","path":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md"},"output":"Edited specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md (1 replacement)","title":"edit","metadata":{"metadata":{"files":[{"file":"specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md","patch":"Index: specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md\n===================================================================\n--- specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md\n+++ specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md\n@@ -70,16 +70,19 @@\n \n - Init: read the brief, the scope file, the two scope files and the pass-ten/pass-eleven reports before choosing a focus; the prior reports name the single-quote and retired-name fixes this pass must test.\n - Iteration 1: exercising the module's exported `commandOnlyMentioned` over the test's own `PLAIN_ROWS` instead of only reading the assertions. That drove the wrapper matrix through the real normalizer and lead tests, so the flip is reproduced rather than argued.\n - Iteration 1: reading the occurrence scan and the lead tests separately. It showed the bracketed name is found and the lead is lost, which separated F001 (strip list) from F002 (occurrence boundary) instead of merging them.\n+- Iteration 2: comparing the two \"is this command named\" readers on the same wrapped prompt. The parenthesized form already worked, which proved the missing characters are confined to `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY` rather than a general markup failure.\n+- Iteration 2: measuring the scan on large prompts (100 KB with one occurrence, 2,000 occurrences in 52 KB) instead of asserting a DoS; both are linear and cheap.\n \n ---\n \n ## 9. WHAT FAILED\n \n - Init: none.\n - Iteration 1: a first read treated the invariance test's \"every fixture\" comment as full coverage; it is full coverage of the six listed marks only. The wrapper set is the boundary, not the fixture list.\n - Iteration 1: the `PLAIN_ROWS` omission of the or-list question, bare-lead, pronoun and later-clause rows looked like a separate coverage gap, but all those rows show 0 mismatches under the covered wrappers, so it was ruled out rather than reported.\n+- Iteration 2: the deep-loop half of F003 is masked whenever the prompt also carries depth wording, so the first probe (`**/deep:review** the diff with a thorough pass`) offered the command and had to be narrowed to a short typed request before the loss showed.\n \n ---\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n","status":"modified","additions":3,"deletions":0}],"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Edited specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/review/lineages/mention-12/deep-review-strategy.md (1 replacement)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"newString\":\"- Iteration 1: reading the occurrence scan and the lead tests separately. It showed the bracketed name is found and the lead is lost, which separated F001 (strip list) from F002 (occurrence boundary) instead of merging them.\\n- Iteration 2: comparing the two \\\"is this command named\\\" readers on the same wrapped prompt. The parenthesized form already worked, which proved the missing characters are confined to `COMMAND_START_BOUNDARY` rather than a general markup failure.\\n- Iteration 2: measuring the scan on large prompts (100 KB with one occurrence, 2,000 occurrences in 52 KB) instead of asserting a DoS; both are linear and cheap.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. WHAT FAILED\\n\\n- Init: none.\\n- Iteration 1: a first read treated the invariance test's \\\"every fixture\\\" comment as full coverage; it is full coverage of the six listed marks only. The wrapper set is the boundary, not the fixture list.\\n- Iteration 1: the `PLAIN_ROWS` omission of the or-list question, bare-lead, pronoun and later-clause rows looked like a separate coverage gap, but all those rows show 0 mismatches under the covered wrappers, so it was ruled out rather than reported.\\n- Iteration 2: the deep-loop half of F003 is masked whenever the prompt also carries depth wording, so the first probe (`**/deep:review** the diff with a thorough pass`) offered the command and had to be narrowed to a short typed request before the loss showed.\",\"oldString\":\"- Iteration 1: reading the occurrence scan and the lead tests separately. It showed the bracketed name is found and the lead is lost, which separated F001 (strip list) from F002 (occurrence boundary) instead of merging them.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. WHAT FAILED\\n\\n- Init: none.\\n- Iteration 1: a first read treated the invariance test's \\\"every fixture\\\" comment as full coverage; it is full coverage of the six listed marks only. The wrapper set is the boundary, not the fixture list.\\n- Iteration 1: the `PLAIN_ROWS` omission of the or-list question, bare-lead, pronoun and later-clause rows looked like a separate coverage gap, but all those rows show 0 mismatches under the covered wrappers, so it was ruled out rather than reported.\",\"path\":\"specs/system-skill-advi